  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 115 PM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An increasingly active pattern will support a \ncouple of episodes of coastal cyclogenesis along the South and \nMiddle Atlantic coasts, and associated chances of rain in cntl NC, \nlate this weekend and again mid to late next week. \n\nQuiet weather will persist through at least midday Saturday under \nhigh pressure. This will result in mild afternoon high temperatures \nthe next several days and cool to chilly morning lows.\n\nThe main story continues to be a developing coastal low that could \ntake on tropical characteristics later this weekend into early next \nweek. While considerable uncertainty remains in the track and \nintensity of the developing system, there are some signals starting \nto emerge for central NC weather. The first is that the best chance \nof rainfall appears to be centered on Saturday night through Sunday, \nwith any impacts expected to wane by Sunday night or early Monday \nmorning. \n\nSome of the 12z guidance has come in slightly west with the track of \nthe coastal low and possible tropical development. The GFS/RRFS have \nprobably the most notable westward trend, bringing the system inland \nover central NC Sunday morning. These solutions appear to be \nwestward outliers, perhaps due to convective feedback influencing \nthe solutions. The EC-AIFS has more of a broad low pressure tracking \ninland from FL into the SE US, resulting in widespread rain but \nlimited impacts. The majority of GEFS/EPS ensemble members take the \nsystem along or just east of the NC/SC coast. There are also a few \nfar eastern outliers, such as the CMC, which result in limited if \nany rain. Thus, the consensus so far favors precipitation for \ncentral NC, but nothing significant. Part of the discrepancy leading \nto model spread may be due to low pressure over the central Gulf, \nwhich may be complicating the steering flow and downstream influence.\n\nRight now, our forecast indicates a tenth of an inch of rain over \nthe western Piedmont, and close to one half to three quarters of an \ninch from the Triangle eastward. However, these amounts are subject \nto change until we can get a better handle on the track of the \nsystem.\n\nAfter the system exits early next week, there could be another \ndeveloping coastal low in the late Wednesday to Thursday time \nperiod. Much of the guidance is indicating this active pattern, but \ntime will tell if the system stays off the coast or tracks further \ninland.",
